<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsinghua Bamboo Slips</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tsinghua manuscripts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tsinghua Bamboo Slips are a collection of about 2,500 bamboo strips inscribed in the Chu script in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, acquired by Tsinghua University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 2008 after being looted from a tomb. Radiocarbon-dated to about 300 BCE, they include lost chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, a historical chronicle and the Chu ju, an account of the residences of the early Chu rulers.</p></div>
